✅ Approach 1: Using 3 separate checks (Row, Column, Box)

This part of your code is commented out, but let’s break down how it works.

Step 1: Validate each row

Loop from row 0 to 8.

Use a fresh HashSet<Character> for each row.

For each column in that row:

Skip '.'.

If the number already exists in the set → invalid.

Otherwise add it.

This ensures there is no duplicate in any row.

Step 2: Validate each column

Loop from column 0 to 8.

Use a fresh HashSet<Character> for the column.

For each row in that column:

Skip '.'.

If duplicate found, return false.

This checks each column for duplicates.

Step 3: Validate each 3x3 box

A Sudoku grid has 9 boxes, each starting at:

(0,0) (0,3) (0,6)
(3,0) (3,3) (3,6)
(6,0) (6,3) (6,6)


Loop over boxes by stepping rows: sr = 0,3,6

Loop over boxes by stepping columns: sc = 0,3,6

Create a HashSet for the box.

For each cell inside the 3x3 area:

Skip dot

Check for duplicates

Add number to set

If any duplicate appears, return false.

Step 4 : If all checks pass

Return true.

✅ Approach 2: Using a Single HashSet to Track All Rules

(This is the shorter and cleaner version you kept active.)

Main idea

Instead of maintaining 3 separate sets for row, column, and box, you create one global set and store unique strings that represent rules.

Example stored values:

"5 in row 3"
"5 in col 4"
"5 in box 1-0"


If any of these appear again, the board is invalid.

Step-by-Step Breakdown
Step 1: Initialize a set
Set<String> set = new HashSet<>();


This will store unique validation tags.

Step 2: Traverse entire board

Two nested loops:

for (i = 0; i < 9; i++)
    for (j = 0; j < 9; j++)


You visit every cell.

Step 3: Skip empty cells
if (board[i][j] == '.') continue;


Empty cells do not cause conflicts.

Step 4: Create identifiers

For each filled cell:

char num = board[i][j];


Now build identifiers:

Row identifier
num + " in row " + i

Column identifier
num + " in col " + j

Box identifier

Box index is determined by dividing row and column by 3:

num + " in box " + (i/3) + "-" + (j/3)

Step 5: Check if any identifier already exists

If the number is already present in the same row, column, or box:

if (set.contains(row) || set.contains(col) || set.contains(box))
    return false;


Immediate failure.

Step 6: Add the identifiers to the set
set.add(row);
set.add(col);
set.add(box);


This helps catch duplicates later.

Step 7: If no conflicts found

After scanning entire grid:

return true;


Board is valid.

🟢 Summary of Both Approaches
Approach	How it works	Pros	Cons
3 separate checks	Check row, column, box separately	Clear logic	More code
Single HashSet	Use strings to track all rules	Short, clean, fast	Slightly more memory for strings